---
/*
 * Building Report Card: what the terms mean, where every section comes
 * from, and what the records can't tell you.
 */
import { CLASSES, DATASETS, LOT_DATASETS, datasetUrl } from '../../lib/building-report/config.js';
const sources = [...Object.values(DATASETS), ...Object.values(LOT_DATASETS)];
---

<section class="dash-section" aria-labelledby="br-terms-title">
  <header class="section-head">
    <h2 id="br-terms-title">What the terms mean</h2>
  </header>
  <dl class="br-terms">
    <div><dt>HPD and DOB</dt><dd>The two city agencies with records on a building. HPD (Housing Preservation and Development) enforces the housing code in rentals: heat, leaks, pests, paint. DOB (the Department of Buildings) covers the structure: construction, elevators, boilers, gas, façades.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Repair order (violation)</dt><dd>A problem an HPD inspector saw and ordered the owner to fix. HPD sorts them by how serious they are:</dd></div>
    {CLASSES.map((c) => (
      <div class="br-term-sub"><dt>{c.label} ({c.code})</dt><dd>{c.text}</dd></div>
    ))}
    <div><dt>Vacate order</dt><dd>An order to leave all or part of a building because it isn’t safe to live in, often after a fire.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>The city’s watch list (AEP)</dt><dd>Each year HPD picks the buildings with the most serious open problems for its Alternative Enforcement Program. The owner has to fix the underlying problems (not just patch them); if they don’t, HPD can do the work and bill them. A building leaves (is “discharged”) when it meets the program’s requirements.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Emergency repairs by the city</dt><dd>When an owner doesn’t fix an emergency, HPD hires a contractor to do it and bills the owner: Open Market Orders (OMO) for bigger jobs, Handyman Work Orders (HWO) for smaller ones. Unpaid bills become a lien on the building.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Violations with fines (ECB)</dt><dd>DOB violations heard at the city’s tribunal (OATH, which took over from the Environmental Control Board), which can fine the owner.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>No report filed vs. no bedbugs</dt><dd>Owners report bedbugs themselves once a year. A report with zero apartments says the owner found none; no report says nothing either way.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>BBL and BIN</dt><dd>The city’s numbers for a tax lot (borough, block and lot) and for each building on it. HPD keeps records by lot, DOB by building.</dd></div>
  </dl>
</section>

<section class="dash-section" aria-labelledby="br-about-title">
  <header class="section-head">
    <h2 id="br-about-title">Where this comes from</h2>
  </header>
  <aside class="callout callout-heads-up" role="note">
    <strong>Heads up:</strong> everything here comes from NYC Open Data, as the city publishes it. Records can lag by
    days or weeks and can have mistakes. An “open” violation may already be fixed and not yet cleared in HPD’s system.
    This is a summary of public records, not an inspection, and not legal advice. For a lease, a purchase or a dispute,
    check with HPD (<a href="https://hpdonline.nyc.gov/" rel="noopener">HPD Online</a>), DOB or a lawyer.
  </aside>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Each section shows its dataset and when the city last updated it. Pest, mold and garbage repair orders are picked out of HPD’s violations by their wording. Addresses are looked up with NYC Planning Labs’
    GeoSearch, which gives each address its tax lot (BBL). A link to a report holds only the BBL.
  </p>
  <ul class="ar-sources">
    {sources.map((s) => <li><a href={datasetUrl(s.id)} rel="noopener">{s.name}</a> ({s.id})</li>)}
  </ul>
  <p class="ar-note">
    DOB complaint categories are decoded with DOB’s published category list (revised September 2021). Newer codes
    not on it show as “Other DOB complaint” with the code.
  </p>
</section>
